    "Instructions": {
        "Text": "Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, you are instructed as follows:\n\nThe defendants, Michael Kowalski and Diego Reyes, are each charged with one count of Manslaughter under Texas Penal Code § 19.04, and in the alternative, one count of Criminally Negligent Homicide under § 19.05.\n\nMANSLAUGHTER requires the State to prove beyond a reasonable doubt that the defendant recklessly caused the death of James Oakes. A person acts recklessly when they are aware of but consciously disregard a substantial and unjustifiable risk that death will occur. The risk must be of such a nature and degree that its disregard constitutes a gross deviation from the standard of care an ordinary person would exercise.\n\nCRIMINALLY NEGLIGENT HOMICIDE requires the State to prove beyond a reasonable doubt that the defendant caused the death of James Oakes by criminal negligence. A person acts with criminal negligence when they ought to be aware of a substantial and unjustifiable risk that death will occur. The failure to perceive the risk must constitute a gross deviation from the standard of care an ordinary person would exercise.\n\nThe distinction: recklessness requires AWARENESS of the risk; criminal negligence requires that the person SHOULD HAVE BEEN aware of the risk.\n\nThe defendants have raised the defense that they were acting under orders from a superior officer, Sergeant Marcus Cross. Under Texas law, obedience to a superior order is not a defense to criminal conduct if the order was MANIFESTLY ILLEGAL — meaning a person of ordinary sense and understanding would know the order was illegal. You may consider the defendants\u0027 belief that they were following orders when evaluating whether they acted with the required mental state, but obedience to orders does not, by itself, excuse criminal conduct.\n\nThe defendants do not dispute that their actions caused the death of James Oakes. You must determine whether those actions were reckless (manslaughter), criminally negligent (criminally negligent homicide), or neither.\n\nYou must deliberate together and reach a unanimous verdict as to each defendant on each charge. Your verdict must be based solely on the evidence and these instructions of law.",
        "Standard": "BeyondReasonableDoubt"
    },
