            "FileName": "Autopsy Report - James Oakes.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Medical examiner\u0027s report for Cadet James Oakes, 22. Cause of death: asphyxiation due to positional compression of the neck, compounded by fluid aspiration (water in the lungs). Manner of death: HOMICIDE. Key findings: (1) Fractured hyoid bone — the U-shaped bone at the front of the neck, commonly fractured during manual strangulation. The ME testifies this requires \u0027significant force.\u0027 (2) 17 separate contusions (bruises) across the torso, arms, and back — consistent with multiple blunt-force impacts. (3) Petechial hemorrhaging in both eyes — burst capillaries from sustained pressure on the neck/jugular veins. (4) Approximately 80cc of fluid recovered from the lungs — consistent with water aspiration during waterboarding (wet towel over face, water poured onto towel). (5) No defensive wounds on the hands or arms — suggesting Oakes did not or could not fight back (consistent with being held down by two larger men). Toxicology: negative for alcohol and drugs. Time of death estimated between 2:00 AM and 3:00 AM on June 22, 2025. DEFENSE CHALLENGE: The defense medical expert, Dr. Harold Iwamoto, testifies that the hyoid fracture could have been caused by post-mortem positioning — the way Oakes\u0027s body was placed on the bunk, with his neck hyperextended against the metal bunk railing. The fluid in the lungs could be post-mortem pulmonary edema (natural fluid accumulation after death), not waterboarding. The petechiae could be from the position of the head during death, not strangulation. The ME concedes on cross-examination that these alternative explanations are \u0027medically possible but less likely than intentional trauma.\u0027",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-06-23T15:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 1,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.56,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Prosecution exhibit. Strong but contested. The fractured hyoid is the most compelling finding — \u0027significant force\u0027 is required. But the defense expert creates genuine doubt about causation. The jury will weigh dueling medical opinions. No drugs/alcohol simplifies the narrative — this wasn\u0027t a drunken fight, it was a deliberate act.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.58,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Vanessa Chu",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Crime Scene - Barracks Common Room and Bunk Area.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Crime scene photographs and diagram of Building 4, Bexar County Police Academy Training Barracks. The barracks is a single-story structure with a common room (approximately 30\u0027 x 20\u0027) and a bunk room (approximately 40\u0027 x 20\u0027) housing 24 cadets in 12 bunk beds. Oakes\u0027s bunk was in the far corner of the bunk room, furthest from the Training Sergeant\u0027s office. The common room — where the physical discipline occurred — shows: (1) A wet towel recovered from the floor near the center of the room, consistent with being used for waterboarding. (2) Scuff marks on the linoleum floor consistent with a struggle or dragging. (3) A knocked-over chair and a displaced floor mat. (4) NO blood (the injuries were internal/blunt force without skin laceration). (5) The Training Sergeant\u0027s office is located at the OPPOSITE end of the building, approximately 50 feet from the common room, with a solid door that was closed at the time (per Cross\u0027s own testimony). SIGNIFICANCE: Cross\u0027s office was close enough that he COULD have heard a struggle if he was paying attention — but far enough and with the door closed that he could plausibly claim he heard nothing. The wet towel corroborates the waterboarding allegation. The scuff marks corroborate a physical altercation.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-06-23T09:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 2,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.48,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Shared exhibit. The wet towel is damning for the prosecution. The distance to Cross\u0027s office supports BOTH sides — close enough to hear (prosecution: Cross should have known), far enough to miss (defense: Cross plausible deniability).",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.52,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Vanessa Chu",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Sergeant Marcus Cross Testimony.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Transcript of Sergeant Marcus Cross\u0027s sworn testimony. Cross, 47, is a 22-year veteran of the San Antonio Police Department and has served as Training Sergeant at the Bexar County Police Academy for 6 years. Key testimony: (1) Cross admits he called Kowalski and Reyes into his office at approximately 1:15 AM on the night of Oakes\u0027s death and told them Oakes was \u0027a problem\u0027 who needed to be \u0027straightened out\u0027 before graduation. But he INSISTS he meant verbal counseling and a written performance report — NOT physical discipline. \u0027I told them to talk to him. Mentor him. Put him on report if necessary. I never said put hands on him.\u0027 (2) Cross admits that \u0027wall-to-wall counseling\u0027 — physical hazing of cadets — had occurred in his barracks before. \u0027It\u0027s happened. I\u0027ve disciplined cadets for it when I catch it. I can\u0027t be everywhere.\u0027 (3) Cross denies knowledge of any specific incidents of physical hazing under his command. (4) Cross testifies that Kowalski and Reyes were \u0027good cadets\u0027 with no prior discipline issues. (5) Cross admits he went to sleep at approximately 11:30 PM and did not check the barracks between 1:30 AM and reveille at 6:00 AM — \u0027standard practice.\u0027 CROSS-EXAMINATION HIGHLIGHTS: The defense confronted Cross with testimony from three former cadets who stated Cross HAD ordered or condoned physical discipline in the past. Cross denied each account. \u0027They\u0027re lying to protect their friends.\u0027 The defense also confronted Cross with his disciplinary record — three sustained complaints of excessive force during his SAPD patrol years (2005-2012). Cross claimed those were \u0027a long time ago\u0027 and \u0027different circumstances.\u0027",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-03T10:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 3,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.50,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "THE central disputed evidence. Cross\u0027s credibility is everything. His denial is firm but the prior excessive-force complaints and the former cadets\u0027 testimony undermine him. Jurors who believe Cross will convict Kowalski/Reyes. Jurors who think Cross is lying will acquit. The prior complaints (sustained, not just alleged) are significant. His admission that hazing \u0027has happened\u0027 is a crack in his denial.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
