        {
            "FileName": "Kowalski Statement to Police.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Michael Kowalski\u0027s sworn statement to SAPD homicide detectives, given on June 22, 2025 at 10:00 AM with his attorney present. Kowalski states: (1) Sergeant Cross called him and Reyes into his office at approximately 1:15 AM. Cross said Oakes was \u0027a problem\u0027 — he had reported another cadet for cheating, which Cross viewed as disloyalty. Cross told them to \u0027straighten him out before graduation — I don\u0027t care how you do it.\u0027 (2) Kowalski understood \u0027I don\u0027t care how you do it\u0027 to authorize physical discipline — \u0027that\u0027s what it always meant. Everyone knew.\u0027 (3) He and Reyes woke Oakes at approximately 2:00 AM and brought him to the common room. Kowalski admits he slapped Oakes, shoved him against the wall several times, and held him in a chokehold. He admits he placed a wet towel over Oakes\u0027s face and poured water from a canteen onto it. (4) Kowalski states Reyes primarily held Oakes down and did not initiate any of the violence. \u0027Reyes just did what I told him. This was my idea. He was following my lead.\u0027 (5) After Oakes stopped responding, Kowalski panicked. He and Reyes carried Oakes back to his bunk, arranged him to look like he was sleeping, and agreed to \u0027say we were in our bunks all night.\u0027 (6) Kowalski states he did not intend to kill Oakes — \u0027I just wanted to scare him. That\u0027s how it always works. You haze a guy, he straightens up. Nobody\u0027s ever gotten hurt before.\u0027 PROSECUTION NOTE: Kowalski admits every element of the crime. The chokehold and waterboarding are inherently reckless acts. DEFENSE NOTE: Kowalski takes full responsibility, exculpates Reyes partially, and consistently maintains Cross gave the order — at great cost to himself, since calling Cross a liar makes Kowalski\u0027s defense harder (manifestly illegal order is not a defense). His willingness to accept responsibility while maintaining the order defense adds credibility.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-06-22T10:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 5,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.52,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Prosecution exhibit (admitted against Kowalski only, not Reyes). A nearly-complete confession. The prosecution will use this to hammer Kowalski. But the \u0027I didn\u0027t mean to kill him\u0027 line and the consistent claim that Cross ordered it create a path to the lesser charge or acquittal. Kowalski\u0027s acceptance of responsibility may earn respect from some jurors.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.65,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Vanessa Chu",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Reyes Statement to Police.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Diego Reyes\u0027s sworn statement to SAPD homicide detectives, given on June 22, 2025 at 11:30 AM with his attorney present. Reyes states: (1) He confirms Kowalski\u0027s account of the meeting with Cross — Cross told them to \u0027straighten Oakes out\u0027 and said \u0027I don\u0027t care how.\u0027 (2) Reyes states his role was limited to holding Oakes down during the waterboarding. \u0027Mike [Kowalski] did the water and the chokehold. I just kept Jimmy [Oakes] from getting up. I thought Mike was just scaring him.\u0027 (3) Reyes states he told Kowalski \u0027that\u0027s enough, man\u0027 after the waterboarding — but Kowalski continued. \u0027Mike said \u0027he hasn\u0027t learned his lesson yet\u0027 and kept going.\u0027 (4) Reyes admits he helped carry Oakes back to the bunk and agreed to the cover-up. \u0027I was scared. I knew my career was over. I knew I was going to jail. I just... I couldn\u0027t think.\u0027 (5) Reyes expresses remorse: \u0027Jimmy was a good dude. He didn\u0027t deserve this. I should have stopped it. I should have called 911. I think about it every minute.\u0027 PROSECUTION NOTE: Reyes admits participation and admits he recognized it was excessive (\u0027that\u0027s enough, man\u0027) — establishing awareness of the risk (recklessness). DEFENSE NOTE: Reyes tried to stop it. He was the junior participant, following Kowalski\u0027s lead and Cross\u0027s order. His remorse is genuine and compelling. The jury may view him as less culpable than Kowalski.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-06-23T11:30:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 6,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.46,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Shared exhibit. Reyes comes across as a follower who got in over his head. His \u0027that\u0027s enough\u0027 statement cuts both ways — it shows he recognized the danger (bad for him) but also that he tried to intervene (good for him). The remorse is compelling and may reduce his culpability in the jury\u0027s eyes. His account is largely consistent with Kowalski\u0027s.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.50,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Vanessa Chu",
