        {
            "FileName": "Academy Regulations - Use of Force and Hazing Policy.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "The official Bexar County Police Academy Cadet Handbook, sections on Use of Force, Hazing, and Disciplinary Procedures. Key provisions: (1) Section 4.2: \u0027Physical force shall not be used against any cadet except in immediate self-defense or defense of others from imminent bodily harm.\u0027 (2) Section 4.3: \u0027Hazing, defined as any activity that subjects a cadet to physical or mental harassment, abuse, or humiliation, is STRICTLY PROHIBITED. Violations will result in immediate dismissal and may result in criminal prosecution.\u0027 (3) Section 4.5: \u0027Any cadet who witnesses hazing or physical abuse of another cadet has an AFFIRMATIVE DUTY to report it immediately to the Training Sergeant or Academy Command Staff.\u0027 (4) Section 7.1: \u0027Cadets are required to obey all lawful orders from Training Sergeants and Academy staff. A cadet who believes an order is unlawful shall respectfully request clarification. If the order is confirmed, the cadet shall comply and report the order to Academy Command Staff at the earliest opportunity.\u0027 PROSECUTION NOTE: The hazing policy is clear — what Kowalski and Reyes did was prohibited. Section 7.1 provides a mechanism for challenging unlawful orders without disobeying. DEFENSE NOTE: Section 7.1 is a paper policy that no cadet ever uses. In reality, questioning a Training Sergeant\u0027s order is career suicide. The policy exists to protect the Academy from liability, not to protect cadets. The defense will argue the policy was not meaningfully enforced or trained.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-02T14:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 7,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.44,
            "IsDiscoveryComplete": true,
            "ClerkNotes": "Shared exhibit. The prosecution gets a boost from the clear written policy — \u0027everyone knew this was prohibited.\u0027 But the defense has a strong counter — written policies and actual practice are two different things. The \u0027paper policy\u0027 defense resonates with anyone who has worked in a bureaucracy.",
            "MediaType": "Document",
            "EvidenceCategory": "Document",
            "ProbativeValue": 0.45,
            "OfferingAttorney": "ADA Vanessa Chu",
            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Kowalski Disciplinary Record and Motive Evidence.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Academy records showing that Cadet Oakes, two weeks before his death, filed a formal complaint alleging that Kowalski had cheated on a firearms qualification exam by having another cadet fire rounds on his target. The Academy investigated and cleared Kowalski (insufficient evidence), but Oakes\u0027s complaint was known among the cadets. Several cadets testified that Kowalski was \u0027furious\u0027 about the complaint and called Oakes a \u0027rat\u0027 and a \u0027snitch.\u0027 Kowalski was heard telling another cadet that Oakes \u0027needed to learn that snitches get stitches.\u0027 Kowalski\u0027s own disciplinary record shows he was a strong performer nearing graduation and had been assigned to the San Antonio PD upon completion. Oakes\u0027s complaint, if sustained, would have potentially delayed or derailed Kowalski\u0027s graduation and job placement. DEFENSE COUNTER: Kowalski had already been cleared of the cheating allegation. He had no reason to fear Oakes\u0027s complaint — it had been dismissed. The \u0027motive\u0027 makes no sense because the threat was already neutralized. The \u0027snitches get stitches\u0027 comment was barracks trash talk, not a genuine threat.",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-02T15:30:00",
