            "IsOfferedByPlaintiffSide": true
        },
        {
            "FileName": "Defense Expert - Police Training Culture Analysis.pdf",
            "FilePath": "",
            "Summary": "Expert testimony from Dr. Catherine Okonkwo (no relation to defense counsel), a criminologist at Texas State University who has studied police training culture for 15 years and published three books on the subject. Key testimony: (1) Paramilitary police training environments systematically suppress independent moral judgment. Recruits are conditioned to obey without question — \u0027the Training Sergeant is God.\u0027 (2) \u0027Wall-to-wall counseling\u0027 and informal hazing are endemic in police training — Dr. Okonkwo\u0027s research found that approximately 40% of police cadets nationwide experience some form of physical hazing during training. (3) The \u0027code of silence\u0027 (never report a fellow officer/cadet) is taught from day one — explicitly and implicitly. Reporting misconduct is viewed as the ultimate betrayal. (4) Cadets who witness misconduct and do not report it are not acting out of malice — they are acting out of a survival instinct cultivated by the training environment. (5) Dr. Okonkwo reviewed the facts of this case and opined that Kowalski and Reyes\u0027s behavior was \u0027entirely consistent with the psychological profile of cadets operating within a toxic paramilitary training culture — they were doing what they believed they were supposed to do.\u0027 CROSS-EXAMINATION: The prosecution challenged Dr. Okonkwo on whether her research \u0027excuses\u0027 violence. She responded: \u0027I don\u0027t excuse it. I explain it. The question of criminal culpability is for the jury. My role is to help the jury understand why good people in bad systems do bad things.\u0027",
            "AdmittedAt": "2025-07-04T09:00:00",
            "ExhibitNumber": 9,
            "EvidenceStrength": 0.54,
